A call. A call! You always
have to make a stinking call.


00:30:17.274 --> 00:30:20.569
If I was an O.B. would I have
to listen to "a baby, a baby",


00:30:20.611 --> 00:30:23.322
you always have to deliver a
stinking baby"? It's what I do.


00:30:23.364 --> 00:30:25.950
You think I wanted
to miss tonight?


00:30:25.991 --> 00:30:27.618
That I like making those calls?


00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:30.454
I know you like making
those calls!


00:30:30.496 --> 00:30:33.040
Sometimes I feel like you don't
really live here,


00:30:33.082 --> 00:30:34.500
you just sleep here.


00:30:34.542 --> 00:30:37.711
- Like a favorite song...
- It's sad.


00:30:37.753 --> 00:30:41.090
It's just Halloween, Elise.


00:30:41.131 --> 00:30:43.342
How sad can a bunch of dressed-
up kids...


00:30:43.384 --> 00:30:45.219
with a bellyful
of candy actually be?


00:30:45.261 --> 00:30:49.974
Sad for you, Dane.
You're missing it.


00:30:50.015 --> 00:30:52.226
Even when you're here
you're not really here.


00:30:52.268 --> 00:30:55.771
You're always thinking about
some deal you've got going,


00:30:55.813 --> 00:30:57.773
or some candidate
you're scamming.


00:30:57.815 --> 00:31:02.653
One day, you're gonna wish
you had this time back.


00:31:02.695 --> 00:31:06.949
I love you and it makes me sad.
For you.


00:31:06.991 --> 00:31:10.160
I'm tired, Elise.


00:31:10.202 --> 00:31:13.080
What do you say we list my flaws
in the morning?


00:31:28.012 --> 00:31:31.056
You know, I work damn hard
for this family.


00:31:31.098 --> 00:31:34.268
I bust my ass seventy hours
a week in that boiler room


00:31:34.310 --> 00:31:36.854
to make enough money
so that you can stay at home.


00:31:36.895 --> 00:31:38.439
And I appreciate...


00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:40.149
You think it's easy
working commission sales?


00:31:40.190 --> 00:31:43.360
Cold calling? Starting
with a blank slate


00:31:43.402 --> 00:31:46.196
the first of every month?
Waking up at 2 AM


00:31:46.238 --> 00:31:49.992
with a knot in my gut hoping
to God I can close a deal?


00:31:50.034 --> 00:31:53.537
And all so my family
can have a nice life.


00:31:53.579 --> 00:31:56.332
And if that means
missing a fringe holiday


00:31:56.373 --> 00:31:58.834
and being the bad guy,
then that's a sacrifice


00:31:58.876 --> 00:32:00.878
I'm happy to make!
Dane...


00:32:00.919 --> 00:32:05.841
Dane...
I'm a goddamned American hero.


00:32:09.928 --> 00:32:13.098
You did not just call yourself
an American hero.


00:32:16.685 --> 00:32:19.813
I oversold it, didn't I?


00:32:23.817 --> 00:32:26.904
I can't even stay mad at you!


00:32:26.945 --> 00:32:30.032
You're such an asshole
sometimes!


00:32:30.074 --> 00:32:34.370
Ow! Sometimes? Whoa!


00:32:34.411 --> 00:32:38.123
Get over here, American hero!


00:32:45.047 --> 00:32:48.425
Mrs. Jensen, your
pediatrician was correct


00:32:48.467 --> 00:32:50.928
to act so expeditiously.


00:32:50.969 --> 00:32:54.264
Ryan's blood tests show a
platelet level alarmingly low.


00:32:54.306 --> 00:32:57.267
We need to conduct
blood tests to determine...


00:32:57.309 --> 00:32:58.644
Wait a minute, wait a minute,
wait a minute...


00:32:58.686 --> 00:33:00.896
don't you think we might
just be


00:33:00.938 --> 00:33:03.607
jumping the gun a bit?
My wife brings him in


00:33:03.649 --> 00:33:06.276
because he's probably got
some bug that he picked up


00:33:06.318 --> 00:33:08.862
from any one of a hundred kids
and we go straight from that...


00:33:08.904 --> 00:33:11.532
to this?


00:33:11.573 --> 00:33:13.659
Let's just step back
a second, alright?


00:33:13.701 --> 00:33:15.327
I wish I had the luxury


00:33:15.369 --> 00:33:17.329
of time to step back,
to be giving you


00:33:17.371 --> 00:33:20.124
the warm and furries. I don't.


00:33:20.165 --> 00:33:24.503
And, more importantly,
I suspect your son doesn't.


00:33:24.545 --> 00:33:27.089
He's probably just...
he's getting fat


00:33:27.131 --> 00:33:28.465
because he sits on his ass...


00:33:28.507 --> 00:33:30.551
playing Assassin's Creed
all day.


00:33:30.592 --> 00:33:33.303
He's not fat, Mr. Jensen.


00:33:33.345 --> 00:33:35.472
His spleen is swollen.


00:33:35.514 --> 00:33:38.892
What?


00:33:38.934 --> 00:33:41.186
My initial examination of Ryan


00:33:41.228 --> 00:33:45.149
revealed petechiae
and purpura, which you thought


00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:47.568
were bruises.


00:33:47.609 --> 00:33:50.195
He complains of
chronic fatigue and...


00:33:50.237 --> 00:33:54.491
You did not notice those signs?
"Signs"?


00:33:54.533 --> 00:33:56.994
Of what...?


00:33:57.035 --> 00:34:00.581
I believe your son has A.L.L.;


00:34:00.622 --> 00:34:03.041
acute lymphoblastic leukemia.


00:34:09.381 --> 00:34:12.509
"Believe?"
As in "guessing?"


00:34:12.551 --> 00:34:14.553
As in "I don't really
know what he's got?"


00:34:14.595 --> 00:34:17.973
"Believe" as in
"based on 233 children


00:34:18.015 --> 00:34:20.225
I've treated with A.L.L."


00:34:20.267 --> 00:34:23.395
You see, the good news is that
it's the most common...


00:34:23.437 --> 00:34:24.938
form of childhood cancer


00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:27.399
and we have a great deal
of experience treating it.


00:34:27.441 --> 00:34:29.860
The realistic news
is that it gets worse


00:34:29.902 --> 00:34:32.446
very quickly if left untreated.


00:34:32.488 --> 00:34:36.325
You... believe
it's this A.L.L.


00:34:36.366 --> 00:34:40.454
but you're not sure,
how could you be?


00:34:40.496 --> 00:34:44.333
Right? He could be fine?


00:34:44.374 --> 00:34:47.002
Whatever his condition,
Mrs. Jensen,


00:34:47.044 --> 00:34:49.338
he is not fine.


00:34:55.719 --> 00:34:59.264
Alright, Ryan.


00:34:59.306 --> 00:35:02.810
You can hop out now, buddy.
Sorry, hospital policy.


00:35:02.851 --> 00:35:05.562
Sure somebody's
not using this room?


00:35:05.604 --> 00:35:08.774
Oh, my bad. Those are Billy's;
he's not with us anymore.


00:35:10.150 --> 00:35:13.737
He went home Monday!
He got better in no time,


00:35:13.779 --> 00:35:15.906
just like
you will, right?


00:35:15.948 --> 00:35:20.577
Hey! Look, Rhino,
you got your own TV.


00:35:20.619 --> 00:35:23.288
Better than being at home, huh?
Yeah, his own TV,


00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:27.084
private bathroom, and oh!


00:35:27.125 --> 00:35:29.920
If you push this, you not
only get to control the TV,


00:35:29.962 --> 00:35:31.255
but you get your very own


00:35:31.296 --> 00:35:33.966
room-service button too, huh.
What?


00:35:34.007 --> 00:35:37.594
I think I'm gonna have to catch
a little leukemia myself.


00:35:39.888 --> 00:35:43.225
Well, I don't know about that,
Dad, but I'm an aide,


00:35:43.267 --> 00:35:46.895
so a nurse will be here any sec.
You guys need anything,
you just holler at me.


00:35:46.937 --> 00:35:47.896
My name's Antoine.


00:35:47.938 --> 00:35:49.398
- Okay.
- Alright?


00:35:49.439 --> 00:35:51.984
See you later, bud.
Thanks.


00:36:09.042 --> 00:36:11.545
Hey, Dad?
Yeah?


00:36:11.587 --> 00:36:13.964
When's Mom coming back?


00:36:14.006 --> 00:36:16.758
She'll be back as soon as she
finishes the insurance paperwork


00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:19.720
downstairs.


00:36:52.753 --> 00:36:55.505
With the insurance
that we have now,


00:36:55.547 --> 00:36:58.091
the cost of my medication
alone...


00:36:58.133 --> 00:37:03.305
is as much as a car payment.
Your dad, he's...


00:37:03.347 --> 00:37:06.391
He's trying so hard, but...


00:37:06.433 --> 00:37:08.769
I'm very worried.


00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:12.522
Thank you, sweetheart,
and thank Greg for me,


00:37:12.564 --> 00:37:16.234
and please make sure you let him
know it's just a loan.


00:37:16.276 --> 00:37:20.489
Okay, kiss the grandkids for me.


00:37:22.574 --> 00:37:24.701
- Daddy?
- Yeah?


00:37:24.743 --> 00:37:26.954
When will Mommy
and Ryan be home?


00:37:26.995 --> 00:37:29.915
Oh...


00:37:29.957 --> 00:37:33.919
Mommy and Ryan are staying
at the hospital,


00:37:33.961 --> 00:37:36.463
but they'll be home soon
when Ryan feels better.


00:37:36.505 --> 00:37:39.091
Hm?


00:37:39.132 --> 00:37:42.260
Grandma Inez is gonna come
stay with us, though.


00:37:42.302 --> 00:37:46.890
You and Nathan will
like that, won't you? Okay.


00:37:46.932 --> 00:37:49.142
Alright.


00:37:49.184 --> 00:37:52.062
Goodnight, sweetie.


00:37:55.691 --> 00:37:59.277
- Daddy?
- Yeah?


00:37:59.319 --> 00:38:02.614
I forgot to brush my teeth.


00:38:02.656 --> 00:38:05.200
Let me see 'em.


00:38:06.868 --> 00:38:10.455
They're baby teeth. They're all
gonna fall out anyway.


00:38:10.497 --> 00:38:13.750
Go to sleep, pumpkin.


00:38:41.236 --> 00:38:44.281
Dane Jensen.


00:38:44.322 --> 00:38:46.283
Hey, Lou.


00:38:46.324 --> 00:38:49.703
It's just that I haven't heard
from you or this Firestone fella


00:38:52.914 --> 00:38:54.374
Wife's gettin' tired of me...


00:38:54.416 --> 00:38:56.084
hangin' around the house
all the time.


00:38:56.126 --> 00:39:00.005
- What can I do for you, Lou?
- Well,


00:39:00.047 --> 00:39:03.258
if you come across any entry
level engineer openings,


00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:05.594
I'd like to hear about it.


00:39:05.635 --> 00:39:07.804
You know, maybe we can both
make some money.


00:39:07.846 --> 00:39:10.390
Companies don't pay us
to recruit...


00:39:10.432 --> 00:39:12.142
for entry level engineers.


00:39:14.061 --> 00:39:16.521
I'm sorry, man. I've been having
some personal problems.


00:39:18.356 --> 00:39:19.983
Everything alright?


00:39:20.025 --> 00:39:22.652
Yeah, yeah... family issues.
I'll figure it out.


00:39:22.694 --> 00:39:26.364
- Wanna talk about it?
- Do I want to talk about it?


00:39:26.406 --> 00:39:31.161
Dane, I've got three kids;
two daughters and a son.


00:39:33.830 --> 00:39:36.291
Two grandkids. I've been married


00:39:36.333 --> 00:39:38.668
29 years, and it hasn't always
been a walk in the park.


00:39:38.710 --> 00:39:43.131
Ah, it's okay, Lou.


00:39:43.173 --> 00:39:46.343
I'm handling it.
I'll corral Sumner,


00:39:46.384 --> 00:39:49.346
and he'll be back in the hunt
first thing in the AM, okay?


00:39:49.387 --> 00:39:52.682
You take your time,
do what you gotta do.


00:39:52.724 --> 00:39:56.853
Don't worry about me.
It's all good here. But, Dane?


00:39:56.895 --> 00:40:00.023
Yeah?


00:40:00.065 --> 00:40:04.444
Every family has its issues.
But you only have one family.


00:40:04.486 --> 00:40:08.198
Like it, Lou. Like it.


00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:11.493
Take care.


00:40:11.535 --> 00:40:14.037
You too.


00:40:33.682 --> 00:40:37.144
- Ryan, look who's here!
- Hey, Dad.


00:40:37.185 --> 00:40:40.647
Rhino, how you doing, buddy?


00:40:40.689 --> 00:40:44.401
Fine.


00:40:44.442 --> 00:40:47.070
He's almost asleep.
This is when you show up?


00:40:47.112 --> 00:40:50.824
Dr. Singh was in earlier.
And?


00:40:50.866 --> 00:40:53.869
And I need you here
to help ask the questions.


00:40:53.910 --> 00:40:55.537
I don't always know which
questions to ask him.


00:40:55.579 --> 00:40:56.872
Alright, alright, calm down.


00:40:56.913 --> 00:40:59.416
I'm gonna go get
something to eat.


00:40:59.457 --> 00:41:01.209
You'll be here a while?


00:41:01.251 --> 00:41:03.378
Yeah. Where else
would I be, Elise?


00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:06.381
I'll be right back, baby.


00:41:06.423 --> 00:41:08.175
Okay, Mom.


00:41:19.311 --> 00:41:21.605
She gone?


00:41:21.646 --> 00:41:25.317
Yeah. Yeah, she's gone.


00:41:25.358 --> 00:41:27.944
She stares at me all day.


00:41:27.986 --> 00:41:31.239
Like one of those vultures
on Animal Planet.


00:41:36.536 --> 00:41:39.122
She's scared.


00:41:47.672 --> 00:41:51.551
Yes, sweetie, well, I'm doing
the best that I can, okay?


00:41:51.593 --> 00:41:54.846
Then tell him, just tell him
I'm gonna be there for lunch.


00:41:54.888 --> 00:41:58.225
Yes, Lise, I'm leaving now.


00:41:58.266 --> 00:42:01.269
Okay, I'll see you then. Bye.


00:42:04.314 --> 00:42:07.025
Yup.


00:42:11.363 --> 00:42:14.241
Put him through.


00:42:14.282 --> 00:42:16.243
Hey! How's Prague?


00:42:16.284 --> 00:42:19.913
- Give me the number, fucknuts.
- Just north of one-fifty.


00:42:19.955 --> 00:42:23.124
Halfway through the month?
Christ, Jensen.


00:42:27.337 --> 00:42:30.799
Kafka was right. Prague just
won't seem to let me go.


00:42:30.840 --> 00:42:34.594
He's much improved.


00:42:34.636 --> 00:42:38.265
- Should I be worried?
- No. No, he's...


00:42:38.306 --> 00:42:41.518
Put Wilson on. I want to make
sure she mailed Bob a copy...


00:42:41.559 --> 00:42:43.478
of his non-compete.


00:42:43.520 --> 00:42:50.360
Hey. I got Ed for you.


00:42:50.402 --> 00:42:52.654
I checked on your Dr. Singh.


00:42:52.696 --> 00:42:55.323
He's a heavy hitter,
one of the best.


00:42:55.365 --> 00:42:56.783
Thanks, Lynn.


00:42:56.825 --> 00:42:58.493
I got you an early
Christmas present.


00:42:58.535 --> 00:43:00.662
In your "throw-down" drawer.


00:43:09.087 --> 00:43:12.048
I'm about to shove November
straight up your ass.


00:43:15.593 --> 00:43:17.554
Wish I'd done that.


00:43:17.595 --> 00:43:20.473
Which one of you meat-eaters


00:43:20.515 --> 00:43:23.101
is getting an interview
today!? Come on!


00:43:31.609 --> 00:43:33.695
Mr. Jensen.


00:43:34.946 --> 00:43:36.906
What's up, doc?


00:43:36.948 --> 00:43:39.617
I've heard that before.


00:43:39.659 --> 00:43:41.995
You're here late.


00:43:42.037 --> 00:43:45.915
I spoke with your wife
earlier.


00:43:45.957 --> 00:43:48.710
Your son's initial response
to chemo is...


00:43:48.752 --> 00:43:51.212
less than encouraging.


00:43:51.254 --> 00:43:53.048
Meaning what?


00:43:53.089 --> 00:43:55.675
We should discuss options
still available to us...


00:43:55.717 --> 00:43:58.219
Still available? "Still"?


00:43:58.261 --> 00:44:01.598
Gene therapy. Using viruses


00:44:01.639 --> 00:44:04.559
to alter Ryan's T cells
and attack the cancer.


00:44:04.601 --> 00:44:07.604
I've had excellent success
in a handful of cases


00:44:07.645 --> 00:44:09.856
using measles,
the common cold...


00:44:09.898 --> 00:44:13.526
You want to treat
Ryan's cancer


00:44:13.568 --> 00:44:16.905
with, what... measles?


00:44:16.946 --> 00:44:20.075
HIV. An engineered form.


00:44:20.116 --> 00:44:24.913
We're not in some
third world country... no offense.


00:44:24.954 --> 00:44:29.501
This is Chicago. We're in
a state-of-the-art hospital!


00:44:29.542 --> 00:44:31.753
What do we need to do to


00:44:31.795 --> 00:44:35.215
in the realm of non-bat shit
treatments-to turn this around?


00:44:35.256 --> 00:44:39.094
Cancer is not a negotiation,
Mr. Jensen.


00:44:40.929 --> 00:44:43.515
Goodnight.


00:45:18.091 --> 00:45:21.636
So, focus on his
multiplication tables.


00:45:21.678 --> 00:45:24.431
Try and make a game out of it.
Okay.


00:45:24.472 --> 00:45:27.392
Ryan knows what to do
with these worksheets.


00:45:27.434 --> 00:45:31.604
Shh, shh, shh! Quiet study.


00:45:31.646 --> 00:45:33.773
Here we go.


00:45:33.815 --> 00:45:36.443
When you...


00:45:36.484 --> 00:45:39.487
If you need more,
I can always


00:45:39.529 --> 00:45:41.489
send them home with Lauren.


00:45:44.492 --> 00:45:47.036
This is from all of us.


00:45:47.078 --> 00:45:49.789
Thank you. Okay.


00:45:49.831 --> 00:45:52.000
Okay, sweetheart.


00:45:56.921 --> 00:46:00.508
Okay. Let's talk about
the Continental Congress.


00:46:00.550 --> 00:46:03.470
Who can tell me what that is?
Excuse me.


00:46:03.511 --> 00:46:06.556
Whose paper towels are those?


00:46:06.598 --> 00:46:08.558
They belong to the class...


00:46:08.600 --> 00:46:12.437
Could we maybe put them
someplace else?


00:46:12.479 --> 00:46:14.314
Mrs. Jensen, they're just...


00:46:14.355 --> 00:46:17.150
I know that the kids need
Kleenex, I just...


00:46:17.192 --> 00:46:20.904
don't think they need
to be on Ryan's desk.


00:46:23.490 --> 00:46:25.783
Mrs. Jensen, no one's
using the desk.


00:46:25.825 --> 00:46:28.077
Uh, sweetie, do you have room
in your own desk for these?


00:46:28.119 --> 00:46:31.456
- Hey, give me that.
- Uh...


00:46:32.582 --> 00:46:36.002
Take it easy, lady! Geez!


00:46:36.044 --> 00:46:38.588
Keep your shit
off my kid's desk!


00:46:48.806 --> 00:46:53.436
Okay.


00:47:46.823 --> 00:47:48.157
Good to go, little man.


00:47:48.199 --> 00:47:51.452
What's going on?
Where's he going?


00:47:51.494 --> 00:47:54.539
209, South LaSalle.


00:48:08.177 --> 00:48:10.638
Come on,


00:48:10.680 --> 00:48:13.725
let's get you inside.


00:48:13.766 --> 00:48:16.352
"Completed in 1888,


00:48:16.394 --> 00:48:20.023
"the Rookery was designed
by architects Burnham and Root.


00:48:20.064 --> 00:48:23.818
It's the oldest high-rise
still standing in Chicago."


00:48:30.783 --> 00:48:33.953
Dane Jensen. Wyatt!


00:48:33.995 --> 00:48:36.497
Talk to me, babe.


00:48:36.539 --> 00:48:38.791
Sure they've got problems
at the plant,


00:48:38.833 --> 00:48:40.668
is there a manufacturing company
in America that doesn't?


00:48:40.710 --> 00:48:41.794
Dad?


00:48:41.836 --> 00:48:45.048
Wyatt? Yeah, hold on a sec.


00:48:45.089 --> 00:48:48.343
Thanks. What's up, buddy?
Daniel Burnham?


00:48:48.384 --> 00:48:52.055
One of the guys who designed
this place? He said this:


00:48:52.096 --> 00:48:55.433
"Make no little plans,


00:48:55.475 --> 00:48:58.686
they have no magic
to stir men's blood."


00:48:58.728 --> 00:49:01.356
Cool.


00:49:01.397 --> 00:49:04.442
Stir men's blood!


00:49:06.361 --> 00:49:10.031
Wyatt? Yeah, listen...
Yeah, I was having lunch


00:49:10.073 --> 00:49:14.410
one day in Talladega with
Richard Petty when... Yeah,


00:49:14.452 --> 00:49:18.581
that Richard Petty. Anyway,
King Richard says to me:


00:49:18.623 --> 00:49:21.000
"Make no little plans, Dane,


00:49:21.042 --> 00:49:24.504
for they have no magic
to stir the blood."


00:49:24.545 --> 00:49:26.297
Well, I'll tell you
what it means.


00:49:26.339 --> 00:49:28.508
It means if you want to
continue


00:49:28.549 --> 00:49:32.595
to work a safe comfortable job,
then by all means stay at Rycon.


00:49:32.637 --> 00:49:34.639
But if you want a challenging
position,


00:49:34.681 --> 00:49:36.808
one that will not only
define your career,


00:49:36.849 --> 00:49:40.186
but define you as a leader
of men, then this...


00:49:40.228 --> 00:49:43.481
THIS... is your chance!


00:49:43.523 --> 00:49:46.693
Well, of course
if the money's right.


00:49:46.734 --> 00:49:49.654
Forget who you're talking to?


00:49:55.034 --> 00:49:57.245
Wyatt?


00:49:57.286 --> 00:50:00.248
I'll call you back.


00:51:10.193 --> 00:51:14.530
Mmm! Mom, I think our boy's
doing 100% better today.


00:51:14.572 --> 00:51:17.074
Some days are good, some days
are bad, but today...


00:51:17.116 --> 00:51:18.868
Is a good day.


00:51:18.910 --> 00:51:21.496
That's right. So, did they
bring your lunch up for you yet?


00:51:21.537 --> 00:51:23.456
Uh, no.
My dad's coming.


00:51:23.498 --> 00:51:24.874
Okay.


00:51:24.916 --> 00:51:26.167
I asked him if
we could go somewhere.


00:51:26.209 --> 00:51:29.587
- Nice.
- Did you want to come, Mom?


00:51:29.629 --> 00:51:32.465
No. No, Nathan needs
to see his mother.


00:51:32.507 --> 00:51:34.842
Besides, it's you
and your dad's thing.


00:51:37.720 --> 00:51:40.139
Are you gonna be okay
if I get going?


00:51:40.181 --> 00:51:41.557
Your dad
will be here any minute.


00:51:41.599 --> 00:51:44.519
Sure.


00:51:44.560 --> 00:51:48.356
Do you know
how much I love you?


00:51:50.525 --> 00:51:53.110
My first baby.


00:51:53.152 --> 00:51:55.446
You made me a mother.


00:51:55.488 --> 00:51:57.865
You taught me
how to be a mom


00:51:57.907 --> 00:52:00.618
to Lauren and Nate.
I'll be okay, Mom.


00:52:00.660 --> 00:52:03.037
What?


00:52:03.079 --> 00:52:05.206
Of course you will!


00:52:05.248 --> 00:52:09.877
Silly.


00:52:13.422 --> 00:52:16.592
Have fun.


00:52:58.926 --> 00:53:02.221
You closing a deal without me?


00:53:02.263 --> 00:53:04.932
Huh? Michigan Avenue
ain't coming to us.


00:53:13.608 --> 00:53:15.568
"The Tribune Tower,


00:53:15.610 --> 00:53:17.945
"designed in 1922


00:53:17.987 --> 00:53:20.281
by Howells and Hood."


00:53:22.199 --> 00:53:25.411
Huh. Listen, Rhino: "more
than 100 rock fragments"


00:53:25.453 --> 00:53:28.122
from famous sites around
the world are embedded...


00:53:28.164 --> 00:53:29.707
"into the walls of the Tower."


00:53:29.749 --> 00:53:31.751
Look! The Alamo.


00:53:31.792 --> 00:53:34.712
Look, Edinburgh castle...


00:53:34.754 --> 00:53:38.633
Taj Mahal... Great Wall of
China? Are you kidding me?


00:53:41.677 --> 00:53:44.764
Is that one from
that building in New York?


00:53:44.805 --> 00:53:49.018
Sure enough.


00:53:49.060 --> 00:53:52.146
Where were we
when that happened?


00:53:52.188 --> 00:53:55.316
We?


00:53:55.358 --> 00:53:58.569
You weren't even born yet.


00:54:00.821 --> 00:54:03.574
Someone heard a plane
hit the Trade Center.


00:54:03.616 --> 00:54:06.410
We thought it was an accident.


00:54:06.452 --> 00:54:09.413
Until the second one.


00:54:11.332 --> 00:54:13.918
We all stood and watched
on a TV in the conference room


00:54:13.960 --> 00:54:16.295
as the towers fell.


00:54:16.337 --> 00:54:18.297
Were you sad?


00:54:18.339 --> 00:54:21.801
For the people
in the buildings, yeah.


00:54:21.842 --> 00:54:23.469
For little boys like you...


00:54:23.511 --> 00:54:26.013
who wouldn't see their moms
and dads again.


00:55:02.550 --> 00:55:06.679
Do you have to go
back to work now?


00:55:06.721 --> 00:55:11.100
Yeah... I do.


00:55:22.236 --> 00:55:26.407
Okay, last but not least.


00:55:26.449 --> 00:55:29.452
So nice. Oh,
wait, wait. Hold on.


00:55:29.493 --> 00:55:32.580
I thought it would be nice
if we went around the table


00:55:32.621 --> 00:55:34.415
and everyone
could say something


00:55:34.457 --> 00:55:36.167
that they're thankful for.


00:55:36.208 --> 00:55:38.836
- Oh, that's a great idea.
- You know, it's Dane's house.


00:55:38.878 --> 00:55:41.422
Why don't you go first?
Yeah.


00:55:43.090 --> 00:55:46.385
Well, I'm, uh,


00:55:46.427 --> 00:55:50.639
thankful for...


00:55:53.601 --> 00:55:56.270
Excuse me.


00:55:59.106 --> 00:56:02.902
What a cornholing.
Wow, is she reaming your ass.


00:56:06.363 --> 00:56:09.325
I've probably got
another fifteen.


00:56:09.366 --> 00:56:11.160
You don't want
the job? That it?


00:56:11.202 --> 00:56:13.370
Yes, yes, I want the
job, Ed.


00:56:13.412 --> 00:56:16.332
You like working a desk then.


00:56:16.373 --> 00:56:19.126
You've got roughly four weeks.
Fond of you or not,


00:56:19.168 --> 00:56:21.587
I'm voting with my wallet,
we clear?


00:56:21.629 --> 00:56:23.464
Yeah.


00:56:27.093 --> 00:56:31.222
- How's our boy?
- He's good.


00:56:31.263 --> 00:56:34.225
Thanksgiving?


00:56:34.266 --> 00:56:38.187
Why didn't you ever marry, Ed?


00:56:38.229 --> 00:56:41.774
Have kids, do
the whole family thing?


00:56:41.816 --> 00:56:45.486
I know me. I know I'm
the most interesting person


00:56:45.528 --> 00:56:47.113
you've ever met.


00:56:47.154 --> 00:56:49.782
And the most selfish.
I only want what I want.


00:56:49.824 --> 00:56:53.744
- Ever regret it?
- You mean like


00:56:53.786 --> 00:56:56.372
when it's Thanksgiving
and I'm here at the office...


00:56:56.413 --> 00:56:58.082
talking with fucknuts...


00:56:58.124 --> 00:57:01.043
instead of being surrounded
by people who give a shit?


00:57:01.085 --> 00:57:04.839
Yeah, maybe... But then again,


00:57:04.880 --> 00:57:07.716
you're not flying to Manhattan


00:57:07.758 --> 00:57:11.137
with a $5,000-a-night Brazilian
who's gonna turn your suite


00:57:11.178 --> 00:57:13.848
at the Carlyle into
a sexual Slip 'n Slide.


00:57:15.641 --> 00:57:18.269
See ya on Monday, kid.


00:57:28.779 --> 00:57:32.199
I had to
take it... Ed.


00:57:32.241 --> 00:57:34.076
No, you didn't.
It's Thanksgiving.


00:57:34.118 --> 00:57:35.411
To Ed it's Thursday.


00:57:35.452 --> 00:57:38.539
To you it's Thursday!


00:57:38.581 --> 00:57:42.293
We're your family, Dane! Those
people in that room! Not Ed!


00:57:42.334 --> 00:57:44.795
This is my chance
to get off a desk, Elise.


00:57:44.837 --> 00:57:46.297
This is the job...


00:57:46.338 --> 00:57:49.341
I am so sick of your
"this is the job" excuse!


00:57:49.383 --> 00:57:51.844
Well, fine! You don't want me
to work so much? Great!


00:57:51.886 --> 00:57:54.263
After dinner, we'll put
your resume together...


00:57:54.305 --> 00:57:55.514
and you go get a job!


00:57:55.556 --> 00:57:57.933
Hmm? "Elise
Katherine Jensen!"


00:57:57.975 --> 00:58:01.353
"Ten years' experience
as a stay-at-home mom!


00:58:01.395 --> 00:58:05.191
Skilled in potty training,
play dates, and Pinterest!"


00:58:05.232 --> 00:58:08.360
Oh yeah, the job market's
just gonna snap you up!


00:58:08.402 --> 00:58:10.863
Hey, maybe you can go down
to St. Cecilia's,


00:58:10.905 --> 00:58:13.073
see if they'll buy
that "Doctor Mom" bullshit...


00:58:13.115 --> 00:58:14.450
and put you on the payroll.


00:58:14.491 --> 00:58:17.411
Then you can spring
for the cranberry sauce,


00:58:17.453 --> 00:58:20.915
or the white quartz
you just had to have,


00:58:20.956 --> 00:58:22.917
or the clothes you wear,


00:58:22.958 --> 00:58:25.669
or, or the ten grand "loan"


00:58:25.711 --> 00:58:27.379
we gave your brother
two years ago...


00:58:27.421 --> 00:58:29.632
that he seems to have
forgotten about.


00:58:29.673 --> 00:58:33.052
Hell, Elise, look how lucky
we are; we don't ever have


00:58:33.093 --> 00:58:34.595
to tell the kids there's
no Santa...


00:58:34.637 --> 00:58:36.889
because as long
as I can magically continue


00:58:36.931 --> 00:58:39.225
to pull the mortgage out
of my ass,


00:58:39.266 --> 00:58:41.518
I'm Santa fucking Claus
365 days a year!


00:58:54.198 --> 00:58:58.369
I lost the month, Elise.


00:58:58.410 --> 00:59:01.121
I took October,


00:59:01.163 --> 00:59:03.666
lost November.


00:59:03.707 --> 00:59:06.293
Driving around Chicago,
touring buildings


00:59:06.335 --> 00:59:08.879
instead of closing deals.


00:59:08.921 --> 00:59:11.674
Now I've got December to get
Ed's job,


00:59:11.715 --> 00:59:17.054
and Christmas to New Year's
is a deal-killing nightmare.


00:59:21.684 --> 00:59:25.437
I'm sorry that you think
I've taken you for granted.


00:59:28.065 --> 00:59:31.694
I know you work hard for us.


00:59:31.735 --> 00:59:35.489
And you're probably right, Dane;


00:59:35.531 --> 00:59:39.285
the job that I could find
is not what you or Ed


00:59:39.326 --> 00:59:41.996
would consider worthwhile.


00:59:42.037 --> 00:59:45.916
My skills are not
what anyone


00:59:45.958 --> 00:59:49.336
would consider "marketable."


00:59:53.257 --> 00:59:56.969
But I know how to prioritize.


01:00:18.991 --> 01:00:21.201
You okay over there?


01:00:21.243 --> 01:00:23.037
No.


01:00:58.030 --> 01:01:02.326
Holy crap.
Words right outta my mouth.


01:01:22.888 --> 01:01:27.643
- What do we know?
- The designer is Frank Gehry.


01:01:27.684 --> 01:01:30.646
The guy also did
a museum in Spain,


01:01:30.687 --> 01:01:34.191
and he probably did
some other stuff too...


01:01:34.233 --> 01:01:38.195
But I do remember
a quote of his.


01:01:38.237 --> 01:01:42.366
"When everybody else
is ready for the ending,


01:01:42.408 --> 01:01:44.701
I'm just ready to begin."


01:01:44.743 --> 01:01:48.831
I like Frank.


01:01:48.872 --> 01:01:52.876
Me too, buddy.


01:01:52.918 --> 01:01:55.587
Hey, Dad?


01:01:55.629 --> 01:01:58.298
Yeah?


01:01:58.340 --> 01:02:02.428
Do you believe in God?


01:02:06.432 --> 01:02:09.601
Depends on the month
I'm having.


01:02:09.643 --> 01:02:12.479
Me too.


01:02:18.861 --> 01:02:22.114
Did you have a girlfriend
when you were as old as me?


01:02:24.032 --> 01:02:27.953
Why do you ask?


01:02:27.995 --> 01:02:32.499
Cause there's this girl
in my class. Her name


01:02:32.541 --> 01:02:34.793
is Shawna.


01:02:34.835 --> 01:02:38.839
I think she likes me.


01:02:41.049 --> 01:02:43.343
Shawna, huh?


01:02:43.385 --> 01:02:46.096
Sounds exotic.


01:02:50.517 --> 01:02:53.979
He'll get worse
before he gets better...


01:02:54.021 --> 01:02:55.272
Worse?! How can he...


01:02:55.314 --> 01:02:57.149
Ryan's cancer is
unusually aggressive.


01:02:57.191 --> 01:02:58.358
- Virus!
- HIV.


01:02:58.400 --> 01:03:01.069
He's telling us our boy's
going to die.


01:03:01.111 --> 01:03:03.071
Don't you get it? Jesus, Dane,
what are we going to do?


01:03:03.113 --> 01:03:04.948
Cancer is not a negotiation,
Mr. Jensen.


01:03:11.079 --> 01:03:12.789
Just you, it's a termination.


01:03:12.831 --> 01:03:14.791
I told you that before
the interview.


01:03:18.921 --> 01:03:20.506
How do you not pass a drug test?


01:03:20.547 --> 01:03:22.799
When I started recruiting
on this, you told me


01:03:22.841 --> 01:03:24.259
[there were five must-haves
for this job.


01:03:33.393 --> 01:03:35.646
Would you ask Lou


01:03:35.687 --> 01:03:37.940
to call me when he's...


01:03:37.981 --> 01:03:40.734
Uh, what kind of meeting is it?


01:03:40.776 --> 01:03:44.196
He got a call from another
head... from another recruiter.


01:03:44.238 --> 01:03:47.115
Bernadine?


01:03:47.157 --> 01:03:48.242
Hello?


01:03:48.283 --> 01:03:50.285
Dane.


01:03:50.327 --> 01:03:54.206
- Bernadine? Hello?
- Sumner tells me


01:03:54.248 --> 01:03:55.874
Lou has an interview?
That's wonderful!


01:03:55.916 --> 01:03:58.168
You're not upset?


01:03:58.210 --> 01:04:00.045
[Upset? I'll be the first one
to congratulate him,


01:04:01.630 --> 01:04:04.466
You're joking, right?
Listen up, monkeys,


01:04:04.508 --> 01:04:08.095
I need fresh meat to peddle,
stat! Process Engineer.


01:04:08.136 --> 01:04:11.223
Oh, he is very excited, Dane.


01:04:11.265 --> 01:04:14.059
It's an Engineering
Manager spot.


01:04:14.101 --> 01:04:18.146
That's fantastic! Scratch
that! Engineering Manager!


01:04:18.188 --> 01:04:21.066
The drive's a little long,
but doable.


01:04:21.108 --> 01:04:24.069
Gimme a drive-in,
423 area code!


01:04:24.111 --> 01:04:27.489
But Dane, it means
we wouldn't have to sell.


01:04:27.531 --> 01:04:30.867
I mean, we're just...
we're just hoping.


01:04:30.909 --> 01:04:34.788
So am I. You're both
in my family's prayers.


01:04:34.830 --> 01:04:37.624
Bernadine...


01:04:37.666 --> 01:04:40.877
What company is he
interviewing with?


01:04:40.919 --> 01:04:42.963
[Now, I'm not a pushy man
by nature, Paul,


01:04:45.799 --> 01:04:47.467
[about a candidate. But when
I told my guy...


01:04:49.428 --> 01:04:52.931
Pardon my vulgarity... ape shit.


01:04:52.973 --> 01:04:56.393
Degreed engineer, really
mature for such a young man...


01:04:56.435 --> 01:04:59.354
No wife, no kids, easy relo.


01:04:59.396 --> 01:05:02.774
But the best thing is,
he knows exactly


01:05:02.816 --> 01:05:04.568
what you guys are trying to do
over there...


01:05:04.610 --> 01:05:06.486
and he wants
to be a part of it.


01:05:09.531 --> 01:05:11.366
I think we might have found
the seasoning...


01:05:11.408 --> 01:05:12.451
we've been looking for.


01:05:14.494 --> 01:05:18.248
But some clichés are clichés for
a reason. Particularly the one


01:05:23.170 --> 01:05:25.797
Eight AM sharp, Mr. Cobra.


01:05:40.896 --> 01:05:43.899
Dr. Singh?


01:05:48.528 --> 01:05:52.741
- How are you, my friend?
- Tired.


01:05:52.783 --> 01:05:55.285
The T cells we treated


01:05:55.327 --> 01:05:58.246
are fighting the cancer in you.


01:06:00.165 --> 01:06:04.503
Dr. Singh? Your hat...


01:06:04.544 --> 01:06:07.547
Why do you wear it?


01:06:09.675 --> 01:06:12.969
I am a Sikh.


01:06:13.011 --> 01:06:16.556
My turban represents my constant
devotion to my religion.


01:06:18.684 --> 01:06:21.186
What do Sikhs believe?


01:06:23.897 --> 01:06:26.650
It's my experience


01:06:26.692 --> 01:06:30.445
that all religions are
some version of your, uh,


01:06:30.487 --> 01:06:32.948
'what goes around comes around.'


01:06:41.123 --> 01:06:44.543
We believe in the dignity
of everyone regardless


01:06:44.584 --> 01:06:48.880
of race, age, man, woman.


01:06:48.922 --> 01:06:51.842
A devout Sikh would protect


01:06:51.883 --> 01:06:54.845
with his own life
the poor and the weak.


01:06:56.763 --> 01:07:00.016
Ryan...


01:07:00.058 --> 01:07:03.520
if I could take your sickness


01:07:03.562 --> 01:07:08.108
on myself and away from you,


01:07:08.150 --> 01:07:10.527
I would.


01:07:23.915 --> 01:07:25.876
Hey, buddy.


01:07:25.917 --> 01:07:28.336
Hey, Dad.


01:07:28.378 --> 01:07:31.047
Are you missing your party?


01:07:33.216 --> 01:07:36.261
Hey, honey, how you feeling?


01:07:36.303 --> 01:07:38.889
I'd like to go somewhere.


01:07:56.782 --> 01:08:00.869
There you go, Rhino,
the Wrigley Building.


01:08:00.911 --> 01:08:04.289
Man, that's a lotta Juicy Fruit.


01:08:04.331 --> 01:08:06.666
What do we know, Dad?


01:08:06.708 --> 01:08:10.128
Let's see what we got.


01:08:12.214 --> 01:08:15.133
It was modeled after
the Seville Cathedral's...


01:08:15.175 --> 01:08:17.260
Giralda Tower in Spain.


01:08:17.302 --> 01:08:20.555
Built in 1921.


01:08:20.597 --> 01:08:25.685
It has 250,000
terracotta tiles on it...


01:08:25.727 --> 01:08:29.105
That's why it's so white.


01:08:29.147 --> 01:08:34.361
That and the 116,000-watt
bulbs pointing at it.


01:08:34.402 --> 01:08:37.906
That clock? The hour hand


01:08:37.948 --> 01:08:41.910
is just over six feet long,
the minute hand nine feet.


01:08:41.952 --> 01:08:45.747
How do you know all that?


01:08:45.789 --> 01:08:49.751
There's a bank
on the fourth floor.


01:08:49.793 --> 01:08:52.420
I was a teller there
for a little over a year...


01:08:52.462 --> 01:08:53.755
when I was seventeen.


01:08:53.797 --> 01:08:57.509
- You worked in there?
- Mm-hmm.


01:08:57.551 --> 01:09:01.179
Awesome.


01:09:01.221 --> 01:09:03.723
Did I know that?


01:09:07.727 --> 01:09:11.273
It was like going
to work in a castle.


01:09:11.314 --> 01:09:15.110
Only seventeen.


01:09:15.151 --> 01:09:19.948
And in such a hurry to be older.


01:09:19.990 --> 01:09:23.034
Get on with my life.


01:09:29.040 --> 01:09:31.960
I'm glad we came.


01:10:00.363 --> 01:10:04.743
Last call at the bar, ladies
and gentlemen. Last call.


01:10:15.337 --> 01:10:19.257
- You clean up good.
- It's the dress.


01:10:19.299 --> 01:10:23.178
- Oh yeah, I see it now.
- Asshole.


01:10:24.512 --> 01:10:26.264
How's Ryan?


01:10:29.351 --> 01:10:32.812
- How long did Ed last?
- About an hour.


01:10:32.854 --> 01:10:34.856
Stretched it out, did he?


01:10:37.275 --> 01:10:40.570
You did an amazing job.
Situation reversed,


01:10:40.612 --> 01:10:43.990
sick child, I don't know...
Doing an amazing job.


01:10:44.032 --> 01:10:47.827
Doing.


01:10:49.746 --> 01:10:52.415
You're solid, Wilson.


01:10:52.457 --> 01:10:57.003
You don't cut corners,
you do it the right way.


01:10:57.045 --> 01:10:59.798
Wow...


01:10:59.839 --> 01:11:03.551
You know, for all your macho BS,


01:11:03.593 --> 01:11:05.387
you're the first person there
every morning...


01:11:05.428 --> 01:11:07.222
and the last one to go home.


01:11:07.263 --> 01:11:09.015
You're on the phone
from the minute you get in...


01:11:09.057 --> 01:11:11.267
to the minute you leave.


01:11:11.309 --> 01:11:12.519
You're the biggest producer
at the company...


01:11:12.560 --> 01:11:14.020
because
you're the hardest worker,


01:11:14.062 --> 01:11:16.314
not 'cause any of that
Dixie Mafia nonsense.


01:11:20.026 --> 01:11:23.571
We're not that
different, you and I.


01:11:25.490 --> 01:11:28.201
- You're so full of shit.
- We're very different.


01:11:28.243 --> 01:11:31.204
- We're totally different.
- Yeah.


01:11:31.246 --> 01:11:34.290
Anyway,


01:11:34.332 --> 01:11:39.254
the month's not over.
I may have a few tricks left.


01:11:41.172 --> 01:11:43.800
No. You don't.


01:11:43.842 --> 01:11:47.804
You're down $20,000 with ten
live interviews on the board.


01:11:47.846 --> 01:11:49.639
Five are wet because your people
are scared of you.


01:11:49.681 --> 01:11:51.474
They're phantoms. I checked.


01:11:51.516 --> 01:11:55.020
That leaves five remaining
shots. Of those, one pays a fee


01:11:55.061 --> 01:11:56.980
that would blow me out of the
water but it's a first interview


01:11:57.022 --> 01:11:58.565
for a VP level position,


01:11:58.606 --> 01:12:00.650
which means it's rolling
into next year. Four shots left.


01:12:00.692 --> 01:12:03.236
Of those, three are interviewing
for the same job.


01:12:03.278 --> 01:12:05.864
Triple coverage, place one,
that's 15,000.


01:12:05.905 --> 01:12:07.240
Which leaves you
with one live interview,


01:12:07.282 --> 01:12:09.284
which, by the way, looks great.


01:12:09.325 --> 01:12:11.953
But the offer is contingent
upon a drug test.


01:12:11.995 --> 01:12:15.248
A drug test he can't pass.
How do I know?


01:12:15.290 --> 01:12:17.876
I heard him tell your guy
about his coke habit...


01:12:17.917 --> 01:12:19.210
when I tapped into the call.


01:12:19.252 --> 01:12:22.756
You did what?


01:12:22.797 --> 01:12:26.092
You thought I was going
to rely on the honor system


01:12:26.134 --> 01:12:28.386
with a man who named himself
after a venomous snake


01:12:28.428 --> 01:12:31.014
and keeps a drawer
full of burner phones?


01:12:37.437 --> 01:12:40.940
Merry Christmas, Wilson.


01:12:40.982 --> 01:12:43.568
Go fuck yourself.


01:12:59.584 --> 01:13:03.463
Oh, Ryan, Daddy's here.


01:13:04.839 --> 01:13:07.926
Hey, Rhino.


01:13:07.967 --> 01:13:09.928
Hey,


01:13:09.969 --> 01:13:13.223
you're gonna be all right.


01:13:13.264 --> 01:13:16.017
I promise you.


01:13:16.059 --> 01:13:18.353
You hear me?


01:13:18.394 --> 01:13:22.982
I... I got the Frank Lloyd
Wright books.


01:13:23.024 --> 01:13:25.860
And we're gonna go and see
the Thomas Gale House,


01:13:25.902 --> 01:13:29.030
you and me.


01:13:42.418 --> 01:13:44.504
Hi.


01:14:58.536 --> 01:15:00.997
Ryan Jensen's
room number, please.


01:15:01.039 --> 01:15:02.123
Are you family, sir?


01:15:02.165 --> 01:15:05.001
Absolutely.


01:15:09.422 --> 01:15:11.216
The ventilator will breathe
for Ryan until such time...


01:15:11.257 --> 01:15:13.218
as you decide to stop it.


01:15:13.259 --> 01:15:15.970
Or until he breathes
for himself, right?


01:15:16.012 --> 01:15:18.264
Until such time as
he breathes for himself?


01:15:18.306 --> 01:15:21.267
We need to discuss your wishes


01:15:21.309 --> 01:15:23.645
when Ryan codes again.


01:15:23.686 --> 01:15:27.982
- When his heart stops.
- Correct.


01:15:28.024 --> 01:15:31.361
What's to discuss?
It stops, you start it.


01:15:31.402 --> 01:15:35.073
Ryan is in a coma. He's tired.


01:15:35.114 --> 01:15:38.284
Okay, we got it, thanks.


01:15:52.215 --> 01:15:55.593
Mr. and Mrs. Jensen.
Few things


01:15:55.635 --> 01:15:59.097
are as comforting
for a child as the sound


01:15:59.138 --> 01:16:03.309
of a mother and father's voice.
Perhaps...


01:16:03.351 --> 01:16:07.021
Perhaps you can give Ryan
the chance to heal,


01:16:07.063 --> 01:16:11.192
let the treatment
fight the leukemia...


01:16:11.234 --> 01:16:14.028
Please.


01:16:22.578 --> 01:16:26.291
Are you kidding me?
"Sound of a voice"?


01:16:26.332 --> 01:16:28.710
- Stop it.
- Surrounded by millions


01:16:28.751 --> 01:16:31.546
of dollars' worth
of technology and he says


01:16:31.587 --> 01:16:34.382
"sound of a voice"!
I said, stop it!


01:16:37.552 --> 01:16:41.139
He's right.


01:16:41.180 --> 01:16:44.267
Kids need their parents, Dane.


01:16:44.309 --> 01:16:46.602
Not just when they're sick.


01:16:49.689 --> 01:16:53.860
Oh, I love Ryan every bit
as much as you do, Elise.


01:16:53.901 --> 01:16:57.280
I know you do.


01:16:57.322 --> 01:16:59.282
I just wish it didn't
take him dying...


01:16:59.324 --> 01:17:00.616
in order to get
your attention.


01:17:00.658 --> 01:17:04.537
That's not fair.


01:17:04.579 --> 01:17:08.333
No, it's not fair.


01:17:08.374 --> 01:17:12.045
But my little boy is dying,


01:17:12.086 --> 01:17:16.007
so I'm not terribly concerned
about what's fair.


01:17:19.010 --> 01:17:21.304
I love you, Dane,
but I'll be damned...


01:17:21.346 --> 01:17:23.014
if I'm gonna let you
keep him hooked up


01:17:23.056 --> 01:17:26.225
to some machine in order
to make up for lost time.


01:18:13.064 --> 01:18:16.192
I'm sorry about Ryan.


01:18:18.986 --> 01:18:22.073
I was with him when he got
his lumbar puncture.


01:18:22.115 --> 01:18:25.410
You know, his spinal tap?


01:18:33.334 --> 01:18:36.921
Anyway,
I'm studying for my RN,


01:18:36.963 --> 01:18:41.426
so I try to expose myself
to as many procedures as I can.


01:18:41.467 --> 01:18:45.596
Plus, it helps the kids, right?
When they see a familiar face.


01:18:45.638 --> 01:18:48.474
I keep their mind off
the needle with questions:


01:18:48.516 --> 01:18:52.103
what's Dad do at work,


01:18:52.145 --> 01:18:55.898
what grade they're in,
sport they like to play,


01:18:55.940 --> 01:18:58.359
favorite food,


01:18:58.401 --> 01:19:00.945
just, you know...


01:19:00.987 --> 01:19:02.947
stuff.


01:19:02.989 --> 01:19:05.700
I'm gonna ask you
another question, Ryan.


01:19:05.741 --> 01:19:08.327
What's your favorite place
in the world, buddy?


01:19:08.369 --> 01:19:10.455
Hmm? Come on,
you're doing fine, Ryan.


01:19:10.496 --> 01:19:12.290
What's your favorite place?


01:19:12.331 --> 01:19:14.792
- My house.
- Your house?


01:19:14.834 --> 01:19:18.212
There's a place on our step
which lets me know


01:19:18.254 --> 01:19:20.298
when Dad's coming up.


01:19:22.091 --> 01:19:24.886
He's the only one big enough
to make the noise.


01:19:24.927 --> 01:19:27.805
He works late a lot.


01:19:27.847 --> 01:19:32.602
But if I leave my light on,
he'll always come in.


01:19:32.643 --> 01:19:35.563
I tell him it's because
I like to draw,


01:19:35.605 --> 01:19:38.149
but I really just want
to see him.


01:19:38.191 --> 01:19:40.985
When he leaves,


01:19:41.027 --> 01:19:43.988
I can still smell
his cologne for a while.


01:19:44.030 --> 01:19:47.074
It smells like Dad.


01:19:47.116 --> 01:19:49.452
It makes me feel...


01:19:49.494 --> 01:19:51.871
safe.


01:19:51.913 --> 01:19:55.750
Anyway, I just thought
you'd want to hear it.


01:19:57.752 --> 01:20:01.589
I think I would if my kid
someday thinks I'm a good dad.


01:20:01.631 --> 01:20:03.883
Like yours must.


01:20:09.388 --> 01:20:13.059
Antoine...


01:20:13.100 --> 01:20:17.063
My kid ever say what I did?


01:20:17.104 --> 01:20:20.024
For a living?


01:20:22.109 --> 01:20:24.862
Help other dads get jobs


01:20:24.904 --> 01:20:28.241
so they can take care
of their families.


01:21:21.669 --> 01:21:24.672
The Thomas Gale House
is important, not only


01:21:24.714 --> 01:21:27.341
because it showcases the earlier
work of Frank Lloyd Wright,


01:21:27.383 --> 01:21:30.636
but it also hints at his
architectural potential.


01:21:30.678 --> 01:21:32.930
Now, Wright designed the home


01:21:32.972 --> 01:21:35.141
just before he left the firm
Adler and Sullivan.


01:21:35.182 --> 01:21:37.810
He didn't leave.


01:21:37.852 --> 01:21:41.355
Sullivan fired him.


01:21:41.397 --> 01:21:42.565
Because of this house.


01:21:42.607 --> 01:21:44.400
Excuse me, sir,
can I help you?


01:21:44.442 --> 01:21:47.862
It's a "bootleg."


01:21:47.903 --> 01:21:51.991
Frank designed it off the books
for the Gale Family.


01:21:52.033 --> 01:21:55.244
See, before, Frank was all.


01:21:55.286 --> 01:21:58.831
Prairie Style hard lines


01:21:58.873 --> 01:22:02.084
and cold concrete.


01:22:02.126 --> 01:22:05.212
He designed spaces
that let the light in.


01:22:05.254 --> 01:22:08.883
Rooms that opened up,
then wrapped around you.


01:22:08.924 --> 01:22:14.597
Made a family feel protected,
safe.


01:22:14.639 --> 01:22:18.809
Like you belonged here
and only here. Home.


01:22:18.851 --> 01:22:22.313
But Frank was also a gambler,
a risk taker, and he knew


01:22:22.355 --> 01:22:24.273
that if he designed this off
the books for the Gale family,


01:22:24.315 --> 01:22:26.859
his boss might find out.


01:22:28.778 --> 01:22:31.864
Yeah, he knew what goes
around comes around.


01:22:33.783 --> 01:22:36.952
But he also knew that
he could create something


01:22:36.994 --> 01:22:39.872
that would live forever.


01:22:39.914 --> 01:22:42.500
Something magical...


01:22:46.003 --> 01:22:49.965
The magic that
stirs men's blood.


01:23:01.727 --> 01:23:04.814
- Wheeler residence.
- Lou, Dane.


01:23:06.732 --> 01:23:10.027
About time you got off your ass
and went back to work, isn't it?


01:23:10.069 --> 01:23:12.238
Christmas is over, buddy.


01:23:12.279 --> 01:23:14.907
We got a week to get you a job
this year. Chop, chop.


01:23:14.949 --> 01:23:16.617
Think of him as a forty-year-
old, Jimmy,


01:23:16.659 --> 01:23:19.245
we'll throw in the other
nineteen years free of charge!


01:23:19.286 --> 01:23:21.080
Hold that thought, Jimmy.


01:23:21.122 --> 01:23:24.792
Dane Jensen. Hey, Toni,
thanks for calling back,


01:23:24.834 --> 01:23:27.837
appreciate it. Yes, Lou Wheeler.


01:23:27.878 --> 01:23:31.298
No, no, no! Don't you get it?
Every other company in the South


01:23:31.340 --> 01:23:33.968
is thinking first of the year!
You see him now,


01:23:34.009 --> 01:23:37.179
you take him off the market
before they get a crack at him!


01:23:37.221 --> 01:23:39.098
Now, what time Wednesday works
best for you?


01:23:39.140 --> 01:23:40.266
Morning or afternoon?


01:23:40.307 --> 01:23:43.894
Lou? Lou, calm down.


01:23:43.936 --> 01:23:46.856
Tell me, what are you saying
when they ask you,


01:23:46.897 --> 01:23:49.567
"Tell me a little bit
about yourself?"


01:23:49.608 --> 01:23:51.777
No, Lou.


01:23:51.819 --> 01:23:55.489
Lou, no, Lou, Lou, stop!


01:23:55.531 --> 01:23:59.410
Christ, brother, this isn't
the biography channel.


01:23:59.452 --> 01:24:02.246
Even I wouldn't hire you.


01:24:02.288 --> 01:24:05.040
Alright.


01:24:05.082 --> 01:24:07.293
Okay. Say this.


01:24:09.211 --> 01:24:12.256
Miss Smith... Toni?


01:24:12.298 --> 01:24:16.886
You know, I've been working
a long time, you can see that.


01:24:16.927 --> 01:24:20.806
And all those years, I've seen
a lot of people come and go.


01:24:22.725 --> 01:24:26.854
I've seen people chasing what
they think will make them happy:


01:24:26.896 --> 01:24:30.691
money, titles, whatever.


01:24:30.733 --> 01:24:33.194
But it seems to me,


01:24:33.235 --> 01:24:35.488
if a man, or a woman,


01:24:35.529 --> 01:24:39.617
can be paid an honest wage
for an honest job,


01:24:39.658 --> 01:24:42.912
and if you can look back
at what you've done


01:24:42.953 --> 01:24:46.624
and feel proud,


01:24:46.665 --> 01:24:50.169
well then, you're happy.


01:24:52.087 --> 01:24:55.132
I know I'm running the risk of
sounding a little old fashioned,


01:24:55.174 --> 01:24:58.219
or naive even, but...


01:24:58.260 --> 01:25:02.056
I don't think you can
ever be too qualified


01:25:02.097 --> 01:25:05.476
to be happy. Do you?


01:25:05.518 --> 01:25:08.354
No, Lou,


01:25:08.395 --> 01:25:11.774
I don't.


01:25:11.816 --> 01:25:16.153
So. Tell me a little bit
about yourself.


01:25:27.122 --> 01:25:30.751
- Dane Jensen.
- Dane, Toni. You know,


01:25:30.793 --> 01:25:32.586
I've never seen a tighter race
between two candidates.


01:25:32.628 --> 01:25:34.463
We'd like to make an offer.


01:25:37.174 --> 01:25:39.134
He'll take it.


01:25:39.176 --> 01:25:42.263
Whoa, pardner... shouldn't I
tell you what it is first?


01:25:42.304 --> 01:25:45.850
Yeah, all right.


01:25:45.891 --> 01:25:48.978
Okay, what, what,
what are you thinking?


01:25:49.019 --> 01:25:50.604
Well, we're kicking up
the pay grade.


01:25:50.646 --> 01:25:53.065
One hundred grand base
salary, full relo,


01:25:53.107 --> 01:25:56.068
no sign-on bonus. Insurance to
start first day of employment.


01:25:56.110 --> 01:25:58.529
Now, it is New Year's Eve and
I plan to start drinking early.


01:25:58.571 --> 01:26:00.447
My math says your fee's 25,000.


01:26:00.489 --> 01:26:02.241
I need that expense on
this year's budget,


01:26:02.283 --> 01:26:05.369
so what do you say we wrap this
up right now. Is he in or out?


01:26:08.539 --> 01:26:12.209
On behalf of Mr. Wheeler,
I accept.


01:26:12.251 --> 01:26:14.545
Mr. Wheeler?
No, no, Dane, look,


01:26:14.587 --> 01:26:17.214
we all loved Lou,
but he came in second.


01:26:18.507 --> 01:26:20.384
Other guy? What other guy?


01:26:20.426 --> 01:26:24.263
Ping. As in pong. You know,
the engineer with the doctorate.


01:26:24.305 --> 01:26:25.973
Ping?


01:26:26.015 --> 01:26:28.058
Ten minutes after I schedule
Lou for an interview,


01:26:28.100 --> 01:26:30.561
we get this resume from your
colleague at Blackridge...


01:26:34.064 --> 01:26:37.192
Just, uh...


01:26:37.234 --> 01:26:41.113
hang on one sec, Toni.


01:26:41.155 --> 01:26:43.115
Bannon?


01:26:43.157 --> 01:26:45.784
What?


01:26:49.204 --> 01:26:51.290
It doesn't really matter to me
which candidate you hire...


01:26:51.332 --> 01:26:53.125
Good 'cause we're hiring Ping.


01:26:53.167 --> 01:26:56.795
Bannon's my guy,
so his deal's my deal.


01:26:56.837 --> 01:26:59.381
I win either way.


01:26:59.423 --> 01:27:01.550
I win.


01:27:01.592 --> 01:27:04.720
What else you got for Lou?


01:27:04.762 --> 01:27:06.847
Position wise? Nothing.


01:27:06.889 --> 01:27:10.434
Plain Jane Engineer, entry
level, temp to hire, anything?


01:27:10.476 --> 01:27:12.519
No, no, and no.


01:27:12.561 --> 01:27:16.148
Look, email me an invoice, Dane,
I gotta run. Happy New Year!


01:27:16.190 --> 01:27:17.942
Wait, who was the better
candidate?


01:27:17.983 --> 01:27:19.443
What?


01:27:19.485 --> 01:27:21.987
Who was the best candidate?


01:27:23.906 --> 01:27:26.992
Are you recording this?


01:27:29.995 --> 01:27:33.707
Okay. Fact is, Lou's
the better candidate.


01:27:33.749 --> 01:27:36.210
Now, I can't ask him his age,
but come on...


01:27:36.251 --> 01:27:38.712
[How many years does he have
before he calls it a day?


01:27:40.422 --> 01:27:44.343
He's not a good investment.
Between realtor fees,


01:27:44.385 --> 01:27:47.680
closing costs, storage,
relo's gonna run us $40,000,


01:27:47.721 --> 01:27:50.099
temporary living another ten,
and then there's your fee...


01:27:52.601 --> 01:27:55.521
Hire Lou...


01:27:55.562 --> 01:27:57.648
and he's on the house.


01:27:59.566 --> 01:28:01.652
I didn't hear you right.
Say that again?


01:28:01.694 --> 01:28:04.029
You heard right.


01:28:04.071 --> 01:28:08.075
Hire Bannon's guy you pay 25
grand. Hire Lou? No charge.


01:28:08.117 --> 01:28:10.244
What?


01:28:10.285 --> 01:28:14.873
Why in the world would you
wanna give away 25,000...


01:28:14.915 --> 01:28:18.043
Because he's a good man.


01:28:18.085 --> 01:28:21.922
He has experience
no degree can match.


01:28:21.964 --> 01:28:25.300
He's made all the mistakes a guy
half his age hasn't made yet,


01:28:25.342 --> 01:28:29.054
but will, and he's learned
from them all.


01:28:29.096 --> 01:28:33.350
Because he won't lie or pretend
to be something he's not.


01:28:38.564 --> 01:28:42.568
Because everything I need,
I already have.


01:28:42.609 --> 01:28:46.530
And because the price
is right, Toni.


01:28:46.572 --> 01:28:51.201
It's a one-time offer,
expires in ten seconds.


01:28:51.243 --> 01:28:53.454
In or out?


01:28:56.415 --> 01:28:59.501
Who was that?


01:29:01.420 --> 01:29:03.505
Lou, what's wrong?


01:29:08.177 --> 01:29:10.721
What would you think
about seeing the grandkids?


01:29:12.639 --> 01:29:15.017
I thought we were gonna wait
until money wasn't so tight.


01:29:15.059 --> 01:29:17.811
I don't mean visiting them,
I mean seeing them.


01:29:17.853 --> 01:29:19.897
Every day, if you want to.


01:29:19.938 --> 01:29:23.108
That was Miss Smith
from LOK.


01:29:23.150 --> 01:29:25.652
They're based out
in Gainesville.


01:29:25.694 --> 01:29:30.157
That's about, what? Half
an hour away from the kids?


01:29:30.199 --> 01:29:32.910
I'm their new
Engineering Manager.


01:29:34.953 --> 01:29:37.331
Gosh!


01:29:37.372 --> 01:29:40.459
Louis William Wheeler,


01:29:40.501 --> 01:29:44.963
I am so proud of you.
Oh gosh, honey.


01:29:45.005 --> 01:29:48.884
Come on now, come on...


01:29:48.926 --> 01:29:52.012
We both knew it was only
a matter of time.


01:29:54.431 --> 01:29:58.936
Well, I have so much packing
to do, and a realtor and...


01:29:58.977 --> 01:30:01.355
Can I call the kids
and tell them?


01:30:01.396 --> 01:30:05.234
- Yeah! I'm gonna take a shower.
- Okay.


01:31:20.642 --> 01:31:23.395
Well, Rhino,


01:31:23.437 --> 01:31:26.398
we did it.


01:31:37.659 --> 01:31:40.829
I don't know what I thought.


01:31:40.871 --> 01:31:44.124
Maybe if I did a good thing,


01:31:44.166 --> 01:31:47.878
something totally
out of character,


01:31:47.920 --> 01:31:51.632
that it would make a difference.


01:31:51.673 --> 01:31:56.887
And it did, I guess,


01:31:56.929 --> 01:31:59.514
to a man I've never seen.


01:31:59.556 --> 01:32:02.434
Probably never talk to again.


01:32:05.103 --> 01:32:07.147
But I did something today


01:32:07.189 --> 01:32:10.817
that you'd be proud of.


01:32:10.859 --> 01:32:14.154
I mean, I think
you'd be proud of me.


01:32:17.241 --> 01:32:20.077
Just like
I'm proud of you.


01:32:20.118 --> 01:32:24.456
Something I should have
told you every day...


01:32:24.498 --> 01:32:26.458
I'm proud


01:32:26.500 --> 01:32:29.711
of my little man.


01:32:31.630 --> 01:32:35.092
And if you're tired of fighting,


01:32:35.133 --> 01:32:38.971
and... and you need to rest,


01:32:39.012 --> 01:32:42.933
well, then I understand.


01:32:44.851 --> 01:32:47.521
I'll even keep Lauren
out of your room.


01:32:50.774 --> 01:32:53.944
Me and your mom...


01:32:53.986 --> 01:32:56.738
we'll be okay...


01:32:56.780 --> 01:33:00.492
We'll be okay.


01:33:20.220 --> 01:33:22.597
Hey, hon.


01:33:22.639 --> 01:33:25.434
How's he doing?


01:33:27.352 --> 01:33:30.105
The same.


01:33:33.567 --> 01:33:36.069
You need to get some rest.


01:33:36.111 --> 01:33:39.197
Let me sit with him for a while.


01:33:39.239 --> 01:33:43.452
My mom's got the kids.
Go home, take a nap.


01:33:43.493 --> 01:33:46.246
Maybe a shower?


01:33:46.288 --> 01:33:49.291
Please?


01:33:52.169 --> 01:33:54.588
I can't.


01:33:54.629 --> 01:33:56.798
I don't wanna be gone if he...


01:33:56.840 --> 01:34:00.552
Dane, if he codes,


01:34:00.594 --> 01:34:04.097
and only if he codes,
I'll call you.


01:34:06.183 --> 01:34:09.519
I'm not gonna
let him go without you.


01:34:49.017 --> 01:34:52.562
Dane?


01:34:52.604 --> 01:34:54.523
I love you.


01:34:54.564 --> 01:34:58.110
What's not to love?


01:36:34.206 --> 01:36:36.291
You're fired.


01:36:38.210 --> 01:36:40.837
What?


01:36:40.879 --> 01:36:43.965
You can't just fire me.


01:36:44.007 --> 01:36:48.220
My place. I'll fire
anybody I want.


01:36:48.261 --> 01:36:52.641
No, I don't mean... I don't
mean you "can't," Ed, I mean...


01:36:52.682 --> 01:36:54.809
Pack up your shit and beat it.


01:36:58.480 --> 01:37:01.858
Fired? Why?


01:37:01.900 --> 01:37:06.571
Because I gave away one guy?
Ed, I've billed out millions...


01:37:06.613 --> 01:37:08.615
Wilson won.
She doesn't need you


01:37:08.657 --> 01:37:10.116
second-guessing her.


01:37:10.158 --> 01:37:11.785
And besides, you're not
the same guy anymore.


01:37:11.826 --> 01:37:15.330
- I... I am the same guy, Ed...
- No, you are not!


01:37:15.372 --> 01:37:18.750
I don't want to hear it anymore.


01:37:20.835 --> 01:37:24.631
Over. Done. Get out.


01:37:46.486 --> 01:37:48.947
You know, I used to say
a relationship with you


01:37:48.989 --> 01:37:52.492
was based only on the ability
to make you money.


01:37:54.411 --> 01:37:56.788
It just sounded tough.


01:37:56.830 --> 01:37:59.666
I never really believed it.


01:37:59.708 --> 01:38:03.461
Shut the door, please.


01:38:36.620 --> 01:38:41.374
No, no, no, no, no, no...


01:39:24.209 --> 01:39:25.919
Oh, honey!


01:39:25.960 --> 01:39:30.674
Dad?


01:39:40.058 --> 01:39:43.228
Rhi... Rhino!


01:39:43.269 --> 01:39:46.815
How you doing?
Fine.


01:39:54.239 --> 01:39:57.117
- Hey.
- It's gonna be okay.


01:39:59.035 --> 01:40:01.121
It's gonna be okay.


01:40:12.590 --> 01:40:15.510
Come here.


01:40:34.446 --> 01:40:37.490
How's he doing?


01:40:37.532 --> 01:40:40.827
His counts are
still coming up.


01:40:40.869 --> 01:40:43.997
He's good.


01:40:44.038 --> 01:40:48.126
Uh, a messenger brought this
to the house. From Blackridge.


01:40:58.011 --> 01:41:00.805
Here's the thing, Elise.


01:41:00.847 --> 01:41:04.017
I didn't take today,


01:41:04.058 --> 01:41:08.438
or yesterday, or the week off.


01:41:08.480 --> 01:41:11.357
I lost my job.


01:41:11.399 --> 01:41:13.443
Ed fired me.


01:41:13.485 --> 01:41:16.488
Oh.


01:41:17.947 --> 01:41:20.658
Thank God!


01:41:44.098 --> 01:41:48.102
What is this?


01:41:53.942 --> 01:41:56.528
Son of a bitch.


01:42:31.604 --> 01:42:35.608
- Daddy!
- Jesus, Lauren...!


01:42:35.650 --> 01:42:37.193
Watcha doin'?


01:42:37.235 --> 01:42:39.904
I'm working. What
does it look like I'm doing?


01:42:39.946 --> 01:42:41.990
Doesn't look like
you're doing anything.


01:42:42.031 --> 01:42:43.867
I'm making it look easy.


01:42:46.244 --> 01:42:48.830
What?
Can we sit in your spinny?


01:42:48.872 --> 01:42:51.374
No, you can't sit
in my spinny chair.


01:42:51.416 --> 01:42:54.252
Please?


01:42:54.294 --> 01:42:57.255
Okay, come on.


01:43:00.717 --> 01:43:03.344
Getting in?
I'm too old for that.


01:43:03.386 --> 01:43:07.140
Come on!


01:43:07.181 --> 01:43:11.185
- Okay! Scooch over!
- Alright, here we go!


01:43:11.227 --> 01:43:15.565
No!


01:43:15.607 --> 01:43:17.817
Kids, come on, leave
your dad alone.


01:43:17.859 --> 01:43:19.444
You gotta get on the bus
in half an hour.


01:43:19.485 --> 01:43:22.906
Come on. Lauren, that hair...


01:43:22.947 --> 01:43:26.159
Do you want the door
open or shut?


01:43:26.200 --> 01:43:28.328
Will someone wipe me
when I'm done?


01:43:28.369 --> 01:43:29.412
Shut.


01:43:56.856 --> 01:43:59.651
Jensen Group Recruiting,
Dane Jensen.


01:43:59.692 --> 01:44:01.152
It's about time you got off
your ass...


01:44:01.194 --> 01:44:02.862
and went back
to work, isn't it?


01:44:02.904 --> 01:44:04.948
Lou, how you doin'?!


01:44:04.989 --> 01:44:07.659
Oh man, I'm busier
than a one-legged man


01:44:07.700 --> 01:44:09.869
in an ass-kicking contest...
And I'm loving it!


01:44:11.537 --> 01:44:14.874
And how about you? Can't be easy
starting over again.


01:44:14.916 --> 01:44:18.252
Oh, I'm... I'm pretty busy.


01:44:18.294 --> 01:44:20.797
Come again?


01:44:20.838 --> 01:44:22.423
Now that I've got my feet on
the ground...


01:44:22.465 --> 01:44:24.842
I've got the go-ahead
to hire some new engineers.


01:44:24.884 --> 01:44:28.680
Engineers?


01:44:28.721 --> 01:44:30.682
Plural?


01:44:30.723 --> 01:44:33.309
Yeah, we're gonna need two
at least to start, maybe three.


01:44:36.104 --> 01:44:37.522
And I wouldn't want to talk
to anybody but you, Dane.


01:44:37.563 --> 01:44:39.607
And I never will.


01:44:39.649 --> 01:44:43.361
I appreciate that, Lou. I do.


01:44:43.403 --> 01:44:47.073
And I'm thinking of one name,


01:44:47.115 --> 01:44:50.952
Mohammed Al Farooq.


01:44:50.994 --> 01:44:54.789
- Oh, boy.
- Hey, Lou...


01:44:54.831 --> 01:44:57.417
You mind if I call you back
in about an hour?


01:44:57.458 --> 01:45:00.128
I've got a daily meeting
I can't miss.


01:45:00.169 --> 01:45:01.546
Walnuts?


01:45:01.587 --> 01:45:03.339
Oz says they're
his food of choice.


01:45:03.381 --> 01:45:05.633
If you're ever stranded on
a deserted island... walnuts.


01:45:05.675 --> 01:45:09.429
Guess he didn't plan
on being stranded with you.


01:45:15.601 --> 01:45:18.229
You save
the receipt for this?


01:45:18.271 --> 01:45:19.689
I think it's here
somewhere, why?


01:45:19.731 --> 01:45:21.065
Alright, yeah.


01:45:21.107 --> 01:45:24.068
Tax write-off... business lunch.


01:45:27.196 --> 01:45:30.575
husband, father,


01:45:30.616 --> 01:45:33.786
last of the romantics.

